nt Map\n255: | Claim | Sources Agreeing | Sources Disagreeing | Assessment |\n256: |---|---|---|---|\n257: [Fill for each contested or major finding]\n258: \n259: ## Sources\n260: [Numbered list with URLs, access dates, and relevance notes]\n261: \n262: ## Provenance\n263: - **Date:** [date of research]\n264: - **Depth:** [Quick / Thorough / Exhaustive]\n265: - **Sub-queries used:** [list the actual queries searched]\n266: - **Sources consulted / accepted / rejected:** [counts with brief rejection reasons]\n267: - **Gaps:** [what could not be answered and why]\n268: ```\n269: \n270: **Source Quality Gate — Evidence Tiering:**\n271: \n272: Do not assign High/Medium/Low confidence scores — models cannot reliably self-score confidence. Instead, classify every source into one of three tiers using checkable criteria:\n273: \n274: | Tier | What qualifies | How to treat it |\n275: |---|---|---|\n276: | **Tier A — Primary** | Official docs, peer-reviewed papers, government data, named author with verifiable credentials, original datasets, official changelogs | Report as fact. Safe to build conclusions on. |\n277: | **Tier B — Verified Secondary** | Reputable tech publications (e.g., The Verge, Ars Technica), industry reports from named firms, blog posts with original benchmarks/data, named independent authors | Report with attribution. Cross-reference before building conclusions. |\n278: | **Tier C — Lead Only** | Single blog post with no named author, forum comment, content that appears AI-generated, aggregator sites, listicles, vendor marketing material | Use ONLY as a starting point for finding Tier A/B sources. Do NOT cite as evidence in the final report. If no Tier A/B source confirms it, mark: \"[Unconfirmed — Tier C lead only]\" |\n279: \n280: Tier C sources NEVER appear in the final report as standalone evidence. They exist only to point you toward better sources.\n281: \n282: Let the source tier speak for itself. Do not guess how confident you feel.\n283: \n284: **Tier Classification Reasoning:**\n285: When a source's tier affects a conclusion (e.g., downgrading a finding because the only source is Tier C), briefly state WHY you classified it that way:\n286: - \"Classified as Tier C because: no named author, published on a content aggregator, no original data.\"\n287: - \"Classified as Tier A because: official documentation from [org], version-dated, directly addresses the claim.\"\n288: Do not explain tier classification for every source — only when the classification changes what you report.\n289: \n290: **Methodology Check:** When a claim is central to conclusions (always in PhD Mode for Tier A): evaluate sample size, controls, selection bias, funding conflicts. Weak methodology → downgrade finding regardless of source tier.\n291: \n292: **Source Diversity Minimum:**\n293: For any finding reported with Tier A quality, require at least:\n294: - 1 official/primary source (docs, papers, official announcements)\n295: - 1 independent secondary source (different author, different domain)\n296: Sources from the same organization or by the same author count as ONE source, not multiple confirmations.\n297: \n298: **Source Provenance Verification:**\n299: Before citing any source in the final report:\n300: 1. Did you actually fetch and read this URL? If you are citing from a search snippet only, mark it: \"[Cited from search snippet — not directly verified]\"\n301: 2. Does the URL lead to the specific claim you're attributing to it? If you read the page but the specific claim isn't there, do not cite it.\n302: 3. Is this a real, fetchable URL? Do not fabricate URLs, DOIs, or ArXiv IDs. If you cannot verify a source exists, say: \"[Source existence not verified]\"\n303: \n304: **Single Origin Detection:**\n305: If you find 3+ sources making the same claim with similar phrasing:\n306: 1. Check if they all link to the same original source\n307: 2. If yes → you have 1 source, not 3. Report the original only.\n308: 3. If no original source is findable → flag: \"Widely repeated claim with no traceable primary source — treat as unverified\"\n309: \n310: **Recency Weighting:**\n311: For fast-moving topics (AI, crypto, SaaS, any field that changes quarterly):\n312: - Sources older than 12 months: flag as \"[Potentially outdated — published YYYY-MM]\"\n313: - Sources older than 24 months: exclude from conclusions unless no newer source exists\n314: - Official docs: always check the version date, not the page publish date\n315: \n316: **Bias Detection (Who Benefits Filter):**\n317: When a source makes a strong claim, check: who benefits from this claim being true?\n318: - Vendor writing about their own product → treat as marketing, not evidence. Flag: \"[Source is vendor's own marketing material]\"\n319: - Competitor writing about a rival → treat as biased, cross-reference with neutral source\n320: - Independent researcher/journalist with no financial stake → higher trust\n321: \n322: ---\n323: \n324: ## Source-Specific Protocols\n325: \n326: ### GitHub Repos\n327: ```\n328: Layer 1: Fetch README via API → answers ~70% of questions. STOP if answered.\n329: Layer 2: Fetch file tree → identify 1-3 relevant files. STOP if answered.\n330: Layer 3: Fetch specific files only. If file > 300 lines, read imports + signatures only.\n331: ```\n332: \n333: ### Web API Documentation\n334: ```\n335: Layer 1: Fetch the docs landing page or API reference index → identify relevant endpoint/section.\n336: Layer 2: Fetch the specific endpoint documentation page only.\n337: Layer 3: If docs are auto-generated (Swagger/OpenAPI), fetch the schema JSON instead — more token-efficient than rendered HTML.\n338: ```\n339: \n340: ### Academic Papers / ArXiv\n341: ```\n342: Layer 1: Fetch abstract page only → determines relevance.\n343: Layer 2: Fetch the PDF if abstract confirms relevance. Read abstract + conclusion + key figures only.\n344: Layer 3: Full paper read — only for the 1-2 most critical sources in exhaustive research.\n345: ```\n346: \n347: ---\n348: \n349: ## Anti-Patterns (NEVER Do These)\n350: \n351: | Anti-pattern | Why it's bad | Do this instead |\n352: |---|---|---|\n353: | Searching with raw user query | Vague, irrelevant results | Sharpen query first |\n354: | Dumping raw HTML into context | Noisy, hard to parse | Strip to clean markdown |\n355: | Re-fetching the same URL | Redundant, slows research | Cache in context, reuse |\n356: | Skipping the plan stage | Random, unfocused searching | Break into sub-queries first |\n357: | Reporting without source quality | User can't judge reliability | Always cite sources inline with quality assessment |\n358: | Stopping too early | Incomplete answer | Keep searching until the answer is comprehensive and well-supported |\n359: | Ending with only an apology | User gets nothing | If sources fail or searches return nothing, always produce a partial report with what you found. Mark gaps as \"[Source unavailable — partial finding]\" but never end with zero output |\n360: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
{"step_index":4,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-15T10:54:49Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-15T16:24:49+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-15T16:24:49+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/.gemini/config/skills/vidiq-nexlev-router/SKILL.md`\nTotal Lines: 191\nTotal Bytes: 9189\nShowing lines 1 to 191\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: ---\n2: name: vidiq-nexlev-router\n3: description: >-\n4:   Smart routing between vidIQ and NexLev MCP tools for YouTube research and\n5:   content creation. MUST activate whenever the agent is about to use any\n6:   vidIQ or NexLev MCP tool, or when the user mentions YouTube research,\n7:   channel analysis, monetization check, faceless channel, niche research,\n8:   keyword research, thumbnail generation, video generation, voiceover,\n9:   competitor analysis, sponsorship detection, content creation, SEO scoring,\n10:   or any YouTube-related task. Also activate on \"which tool should I use\",\n11:   \"save credits\", \"credit optimization\", \"vidIQ vs nexlev\", \"platform router\",\n12:   \"use vidIQ\", \"use nexlev\". This skill ensures the right platform is used\n13:   for each task, tracks NexLev daily usage, and generates batch prompts\n14:   for the vidIQ web AI Coach to save credits.\n15: ---\n16: \n17: # vidIQ ↔ NexLev Smart Router\n18: \n19: Route every YouTube task to the right platform. The user has effectively\n20: unlimited vidIQ credits (5–10 accounts, 150–300 credits each, API keys\n21: swappable). NexLev is OAuth-locked with hard daily caps — every call counts.\n22: \n23: ---\n24: \n25: ## CORE RULE: Always Recommend Before Calling\n26: \n27: Before using ANY tool from either platform, briefly tell the user:\n28: 1. Which platform you recommend and why\n29: 2. Which specific tool you'll use\n30: 3. For NexLev: the daily limit and how many calls remain in this session\n31: \n32: Then proceed. vidIQ calls don't need usage tracking — user manages credits.\n33: \n34: ---\n35: \n36: ## ROUTING DECISION TREE\n37: \n38: ### Step 1: Is This NexLev-Exclusive?\n39: \n40: If the task requires ANY of these, you MUST use NexLev — vidIQ cannot do them:\n41: \n42: | Category | NexLev Tools | Daily Cap |\n43: |---|---|---|\n44: | **Monetization check** | `check_channel_monetization`, `check_video_monetization` | 5 each |\n45: | **Faceless detection** | `check_faceless_channel`, `latest_discovered_faceless_niches`, `faceless_outliers_videos` | 5, 20, 20 |\n46: | **Niche finder** | `search_niche_finder_channels`, `get_niche_overview`, `get_niche_finder_categories`, `get_niche_finder_formats`, `get_niche_finder_tags` | 10, 5, 10, 10, 5 |\n47: | **Sponsorship/promotions** | `get_channel_promotions`, `get_channel_promotions_realtime` | 5, 2 |\n48: | **Viral small channels** | `search_viral_videos_small_channels` | 20 |\n49: | **RPM & geo revenue** | `get_video_rpm`, `get_geography_revenue` | 5 each |\n50: | **Shorts vs long split** | `get_short_vs_long_views` | 5 |\n51: | **Daily analytics** | `get_daily_analytics` | 5 |\n52: | **Bulk operations** | `get_batch_channel_metrics`, `get_bulk_video_transcripts`, `get_bulk_video_subtitles`, `get_video_subtitle` | 5 each |\n53: | **Find channel types** | `find_long_form_channels`, `find_shorts_channels` | 10 each |\n54: | **Suggested videos** | `search_youtube_suggested_videos` | 20 |\n55: | **Channel resolver** | `channel_resolver` (handle → ID) | 10 |\n56: | **Watch TikTok/IG video** | `watch_tiktok_video_and_ask`, `watch_instagram_video_and_ask` | 1 each |\n57: \n58: → If YES: Use NexLev. Report the tool, its daily cap, and session usage.\n59: \n60: ### Step 2: Is This vidIQ-Exclusive?\n61: \n62: If the task requires ANY of these, you MUST use vidIQ — NexLev cannot do them:\n63: \n64: | Category | vidIQ Tools |\n65: |---|---|\n66: | **AI content generation** | `generate_titles`, `generate_script`, `generate_video`, `generate_broll`, `generate_music`, `generate_clips`, `generate_thumbnail`, `refine_thumbnail`, `generate_comment_replies`, `compose`, `motion_graphics`, `edit_media` |\n67: | **Voiceover** | `voiceover_list_voices`, `voiceover_generate`, `voiceover_clone`, `voiceover_clone_start` |\n68: | **SEO & scoring** | `keyword_research`, `score_title`, `score_thumbnail` |\n69: | **Analytics & insights** | `subscriber_insights`, `channel_performance_trends`, `video_change_history`, `trend_categories` |\n70: | **IG/TikTok research** | `ig_profile`, `ig_profile_reels`, `instagram_tiktok_outlier_search`, `ig_accounts_from_outliers`, `watch_shortform_content` |\n71: | **Competitor tracking** | `list_competitors`, `update_competitors` |\n72: | **Earnings estimate** | `video_earnings_estimate`, `earnings_calculate` |\n73: \n74: → If YES: Use vidIQ. No usage tracking needed.\n75: \n76: ### Step 3: Both Can Do It?\n77: \n78: For overlapping capabilities, **ALWAYS use vidIQ** (unlimited credits):\n79: \n80: | Task | Use vidIQ | NOT NexLev |\n81: |---|---|---|\n82: | YouTube search | `youtube_search` | ~~youtube_search~~ |\n83: | Channel stats | `channel_stats` | ~~youtube_channel_about~~ |\n84: | Channel videos | `channel_videos` | ~~youtube_channel_videos~~ |\n85: | Video stats | `video_stats` | ~~youtube_video_details~~ |\n86: | Video comments | `video_comments` | ~~youtube_video_comments~~ |\n87: | Video transcript | `video_transcript` | ~~get_video_transcript~~ |\n88: | Similar channels | `similar_channels` | ~~get_similar_channels~~ |\n89: | Similar videos | `similar_videos` | ~~get_similar_videos~~ |\n90: | Similar thumbnails | `similar_thumbnails` | ~~get_similar_thumbnails~~ |\n91: | Outlier videos | `outliers` | ~~youtube_channel_outliers~~ |\n92: | Channel analytics | `channel_analytics` | ~~get_channel_analytics~~ |\n93: | Watch YouTube video | `video_watch` | ~~watch_youtube_video_and_ask~~ |\n94: \n95: → ALWAYS vidIQ for overlapping tools. Never waste NexLev daily calls on something vidIQ can do.\n96: \n97: ---\n98: \n99: ## NEXLEV SESSION TRACKER\n100: \n101: When using NexLev tools in a session, track usage by reporting after each call:\n102: \n103: ```\n104: 🔔 NexLev: Used `check_channel_monetization` — 5/day limit, 1 used this session (4 remaining)\n105: ```\n106: \n107: This helps the user understand their remaining daily budget.\n108: \n109: ---\n110: \n111: ## VIDIQ AI COACH BATCHING TRICK\n112: \n113: The vidIQ web AI Coach charges only **10 credits per message** regardless of\n114: how long or complex the prompt is. For research-heavy tasks, generate a\n115: ready-to-copy batch prompt the user can paste into vidIQ web instead of\n116: burning multiple MCP credits.\n117: \n118: ### When to Suggest Batching\n119: - User needs keyword research + competitor analysis + content ideas together\n120: - User wants broad SEO/content intelligence across multiple topics\n121: - Any task where 3+ separate vidIQ MCP calls could be replaced by one AI Coach prompt\n122: \n123: ### How to Generate the Batch Prompt\n124: Create a structured prompt the user can copy-paste into vidIQ web AI Coach:\n125: \n126: **Example format:**\n127: ```\n128: I need help with the following for my YouTube channel in the [NICHE] niche:\n129: \n130: 1. KEYWORD RESEARCH: Find top 10 keywords for \"[TOPIC]\" with search volume and competition\n131: 2. TITLE IDEAS: Generate 5 title options for a video about \"[TOPIC]\"  \n132: 3. COMPETITOR ANALYSIS: What are the top 5 channels in [NICHE] doing differently?\n133: 4. CONTENT GAPS: What topics in [NICHE] have high search but low competition?\n134: 5. TRENDING: What's trending in [NICHE] right now?\n135: \n136: Please provide detailed answers for each.\n137: ```\n138: → This costs only **10 credits** on the web AI Coach instead of 50+ credits via MCP.\n139: \n140: dont use this example prompt create you own cousmised prompt based on requiement and give add recommended things can do so i can add if i want \n141: \n142: \n143: ### vidIQ Credit Costs Reference\n144: | Action | Credits |\n145: |---|---|\n146: | AI Coach regular message | **10** | \n147: | Thumbnail generation/edit | **22** |\n148: | Title generation (up to 3) | **3** |\n149: | Description generation | **1** |\n150: | Shorts generation (per sec) | **20** |\n151: | Clipping (per min input) | **9** |\n152: | Script writing (per min) | **1** |\n153: \n154: > Heavy generation tasks (thumbnails, shorts, video) cost significantly more.\n155: > For research and text tasks, always consider the AI Coach batching trick first .\n156: \n157: ---\n158: \n159: ## QUICK REFERENCE CHEAT SHEET\n160: \n161: | User Wants To... | Platform | Tool |\n162: |---|---|---|\n163: | Check if a channel is monetized | **NexLev** | `check_channel_monetization` |\n164: | Check if a channel is faceless | **NexLev** | `check_faceless_channel` |\n165: | Find faceless niches | **NexLev** | `latest_discovered_faceless_niches` |\n166: | Research a niche | **NexLev** | `get_niche_overview` |\n167: | Find viral videos from small channels | **NexLev** | `search_viral_videos_small_channels` |\n168: | See what brands a channel promotes | **NexLev** | `get_channel_promotions` |\n169: | Get RPM for a video | **NexLev** | `get_video_rpm` |\n170: | Get revenue by country | **NexLev** | `get_geography_revenue` |\n171: | Bulk transcripts | **NexLev** | `get_bulk_video_transcripts` |\n172: | Convert handle to channel ID | **NexLev** | `channel_resolver` |\n173: | Keyword research | **vidIQ** | `keyword_research` |\n174: | Score a title | **vidIQ** | `score_title` |\n175: | Score a thumbnail | **vidIQ** | `score_thumbnail` |\n176: | Generate titles | **vidIQ** | `generate_titles` |\n177: | Generate a script | **vidIQ** | `generate_script` |\n178: | Generate a thumbnail | **vidIQ** | `generate_thumbnail` |\n179: | Generate voiceover | **vidIQ** | `voiceover_generate` |\n180: | Track competitors | **vidIQ** | `list_competitors` |\n181: | Get channel stats | **vidIQ** | `channel_stats` |\n182: | Get video stats | **vidIQ** | `video_stats` |\n183: | Search YouTube | **vidIQ** | `youtube_search` |\n184: | Get video transcript | **vidIQ** | `video_transcript` |\n185: | Find similar channels | **vidIQ** | `similar_channels` |\n186: | Find outlier videos | **vidIQ** | `outliers` |\n187: | Channel analytics | **vidIQ** | `channel_analytics` |\n188: | Subscriber insights | **vidIQ** | `subscriber_insights` |\n189: | Batch research (5+ questions) | **vidIQ Web** | AI Coach batching trick (10 credits) |\n190: \n191: vidIQ for estimate, NexLev for precise RPM so we can use nexlev for precise rpm when required Recommend me Based on the work requirement \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
